{"audio":{"src":"https://pub-13abeb101e2945d2a7f60234f6faf52e.r2.dev/materials/b1-explainer-about-australian-culture-for-adult-learners-explain-what-usually-stands-out-w/audio/main-e8926c1a5adf.mp3","durationSec":255.84,"cues":[{"start":0,"end":6.7,"text":"Australian culture begins with Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander peoples, also called First Nations peoples."},{"start":7.58,"end":10.7,"text":"Their cultures are among the oldest continuing cultures on Earth."},{"start":11.54,"end":16.82,"text":"People have lived on this land for tens of thousands of years, long before British ships arrived."},{"start":17.8,"end":19.18,"text":"One key word is Country."},{"start":19.96,"end":26.4,"text":"In the First Nations sense, Country is a living relationship with land, water, and the wider living world."},{"start":26.4,"end":28.84,"text":"It is not only a place on a map."},{"start":29.66,"end":37.8,"text":"Welcome to Country and Acknowledgement of Country ceremonies show that this idea still shapes public life in cities such as Sydney and Melbourne."},{"start":37.8,"end":45.34,"text":"Torres Strait Islander peoples also keep strong sea and island links across the Torres Strait, north of Queensland."},{"start":46.6,"end":50.92,"text":"In 1788 the First Fleet arrived in Sydney, New South Wales."},{"start":50.92,"end":58.9,"text":"Eleven British ships brought convicts, officers, and supplies, and British colonisation and settler society grew from that landing."},{"start":59.94,"end":65.58,"text":"Convict transportation and later free settlement changed land, law, and daily life across the continent."},{"start":66.5,"end":69.1,"text":"From that history came strong talk about fairness."},{"start":69.9,"end":72.5,"text":"The fair go means people should get a fair chance."},{"start":73.4,"end":79.7,"text":"Next to it sits tall poppy syndrome: the habit of cutting down someone who seems too proud or above others."},{"start":80.58,"end":88.48,"text":"Together, these ideas push talk toward equal treatment, first names, and less ceremony than many visitors expect."},{"start":89.86,"end":93.02,"text":"Australia is a huge country, and towns can sit far apart."},{"start":93.8,"end":99.44,"text":"The bush means land away from easy city comfort — dry country, long roads, and small communities."},{"start":100.28,"end":107.86,"text":"Distance shaped practical habits: people plan travel carefully, look after neighbours, and talk often about weather, roads, and outdoor work."},{"start":108.34,"end":111.64,"text":"On the coast, city life looks different but still outdoor."},{"start":112.54,"end":118.64,"text":"In Sydney and Melbourne, beach time, parks, barbecues, and weekend sport fill ordinary conversation."},{"start":118.64,"end":123.7,"text":"Cricket, Australian rules football, and rugby league are common shared topics."},{"start":124.6,"end":129.64,"text":"Distance and outdoor life both push people outside the house and into shared public space."},{"start":130.62,"end":134.4,"text":"After World War II, large immigration waves changed Australian cities."},{"start":134.72,"end":142.32,"text":"The White Australia policy, which had limited non-European migration, was dismantled across the 1960s and 1970s."},{"start":142.66,"end":152.44,"text":"From the 1970s onward, official multicultural policy grew, and new arrivals came from Europe, Asia, the Middle East, Africa, and the Pacific in large numbers."},{"start":153.56,"end":157.04,"text":"Sydney and Melbourne became clear multicultural centres."},{"start":157.04,"end":164.2,"text":"In daily city life, visitors hear many languages and see restaurants, shops, and festivals from many family stories."},{"start":165,"end":172.44,"text":"Food streets, suburban temples and churches, and weekend markets show how immigration remade ordinary Australian city culture."},{"start":174.1,"end":179.02,"text":"Mateship is a famous Australian idea of loyal friendship and support between equals."},{"start":179.74,"end":183.64,"text":"It grew in bush work, wartime service, and everyday help between friends."},{"start":184.46,"end":188.9,"text":"The friendly word mate still appears in shops, workplaces, and street talk."},{"start":189.64,"end":193.88,"text":"Because mateship values equals, manners often feel informal to visitors."},{"start":194.52,"end":198.48,"text":"People may use first names quickly and get to the point in ordinary talk."},{"start":199.1,"end":203.2,"text":"Direct talk usually means saying the main idea without long ceremony."},{"start":204.14,"end":212.42,"text":"Visitors who expect more formal openings can find Australian talk sudden at first, then clear and warm once the friendship pattern shows."},{"start":214.22,"end":215.5,"text":"Anzac Day is on 25 April."},{"start":216.72,"end":226.34,"text":"It remembers the Australian and New Zealand Army Corps, or ANZAC, who landed at Gallipoli in 1915, and later service by Australian forces."},{"start":227.38,"end":235.28,"text":"Dawn services at war memorials, marches in towns and cities, and quiet morning gatherings give the day a shared national shape."},{"start":235.28,"end":242.04,"text":"Visitors also notice brighter public signs: the Sydney Opera House, Bondi Beach, and big sport weekends."},{"start":242.88,"end":245.64,"text":"Those famous pictures travel well in films and tourism."},{"start":246.56,"end":255.12,"text":"Anzac Day memory gives a more serious shared story beside them, so people learning Australian culture meet both outdoor energy and national remembrance."}],"words":[{"text":"Australian","start":0,"end":0.5},{"text":"culture","start":0.5,"end":0.94},{"text":"begins","start":0.94,"end":1.4},{"text":"with","start":1.4,"end":1.68},{"text":"Aboriginal","start":1.68,"end":2.22},{"text":"and","start":2.22,"end":2.72},{"text":"Torres","start":2.72,"end":3.06},{"text":"Strait","start":3.06,"end":3.4},{"text":"Islander","start":3.4,"end":3.94},{"text":"peoples","start":3.94,"end":4.28},{"text":"also","sta